-- USBSID-Pico SuperDirt demo 6/6: "Big Top" - a kitchen-sink showcase over an
-- Em-C-G-D circus/EDM loop. Not a genre demo like 1-5, this one exists to
-- make you HEAR the MIDI engine's own features, not just play notes through it:
--   - multi-keypress:  SID3 holds a real 3-note chord that the firmware's own
--     arpeggiator (not Tidal) cycles through at MIDI-clock speed
--   - multi-patch:     SID1's bass AND FMOpl's hook both swap Program Change
--     mid-song, live, timed to the section change (bar 3)
--   - unison + detune: SID2's lead runs CC_UNIS with CC_UDET spread - one
--     mono voice, thickened, not a chord
--   - dual LFOs:       LFO1 (vibrato, pitch) on the unison lead, LFO2 (wah,
--     cutoff) on the arp'd chord voice
-- All four parts share the same chord underneath, so bar 4's downbeat lands
-- every part on D at once - SID1 bass, SID2 unison lead, SID3's arp'd chord
-- and FMOpl's hook all converge there. That's the whole pool sounding at
-- once, which is as "multi-keypress" as a demo file gets without a real
-- keyboard.
--
-- Ported 1:1 from ../strudel/big_top_showcase.strudel.js - see that file
-- for the full musical/firmware commentary.
--
-- Channel map for THIS user's board (2xSID physical config: socket 1 hosts
-- a clone presenting as 2x8580, socket 2 hosts a clone presenting as
-- 1x8580 + 1xFMOpl): channels 1/2/3 = SID1/2/3 one-SID-each, channel 4 =
-- FMOpl. Same custom (non-firmware-default) mapping as every other demo in
-- this folder - see pop_classical.tidal for the full explanation. If your
-- board's channel config differs, adjust the midichan values below.
--
-- Patch choices (repo/src/midi_patch.c factory bank):
--   14 Wide bass       - pulse ~65% duty, punchy (bars 1-2)
--   3  Bass            - sawtooth, low-passed, tighter (bars 3-4, the drop)
--   6  Filtered lead   - sawtooth through a resonant low-pass (unison lead)
--   1  Sawtooth lead    - plain, clean base for the clock-synced arp chord
--   FMOpl 1 Electric piano (bars 1-2) / FMOpl 4 Bell (bars 3-4, the drop)
--
-- CC map used here (repo/src/midi_defs.h): CC_VOL=7, CC_FMEN=88, CC_ANOF=123,
-- CC_UNIS=41, CC_UDET=42, CC_LFOW/R/D/T=0/2/3/4, CC_LFO2W/R/D/T=13/14/15/16,
-- CC_ARPM/R/O/E=6/10/11/12.
--
-- Uses the "usbsidpico" MIDI destination (a virtual MIDI-usbsidpico port), not
-- the direct "usbsidpico" one. Route that port to the board yourself (e.g.
-- via your OS's MIDI patchbay / ALSA aconnect), same as the original
-- Strudel demo assumed for 'Midi Through Port-0'.

-- CC_ANOF (All Notes Off) is pool-wide - clears every held SID and FMOpl
-- voice before this song sets up its own state.
once $ ccn 123 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 1  -- normalized 1 -> 127

-- CC_FMEN, CC_UNIS and CC_ARPE are all sticky per-channel firmware state -
-- none of them reset themselves between songs, so explicitly force every
-- channel back to a known baseline before turning any of them on again.
do
  once $ ccn 88 # ccv 0   # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 1  -- ch1 SID target (FMEN off)
  once $ ccn 88 # ccv 0   # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 2  -- ch2 SID target
  once $ ccn 88 # ccv 0   # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3  -- ch3 SID target
  once $ ccn 88 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 4  -- ch4 -> FMOpl, normalized 1 -> 127
  once $ ccn 41 # ccv 0   # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 1  -- unison off ch1
  once $ ccn 41 # ccv 0   # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 2  -- unison off ch2 (set on below)
  once $ ccn 41 # ccv 0   # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3  -- unison off ch3
  once $ ccn 12 # ccv 0   # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 1  -- arp off ch1
  once $ ccn 12 # ccv 0   # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 2  -- arp off ch2
  once $ ccn 12 # ccv 0   # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3  -- arp off ch3 (set on below)
-- Volume - SIDs boot silent and need this before anything is audible.
  once $ ccn 7 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 1
  once $ ccn 7 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 2
  once $ ccn 7 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3
  once $ ccn 7 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 4
-- Fix channels to SID's
  -- once $ ccn 104 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 1
  -- once $ ccn 105 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 2
  -- once $ ccn 106 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3
-- Starting patches - bass and FMOpl hook both get swapped again mid-song,
-- see the progNum patterns further down (d5/d6). SID2/SID3 keep one patch
-- each for the whole song.
  once $ ccn 7 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 1 # progNum 14  -- SID1: Wide bass
  once $ ccn 7 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 2 # progNum 6   -- SID2: Filtered lead
  once $ ccn 7 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3 # progNum 1   -- SID3: Sawtooth lead
  once $ ccn 7 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 4 # progNum 1   -- FMOpl: Electric piano
-- SID2 unison: one fat mono voice instead of a chord - CC_UNIS on, CC_UDET
-- sets the detune spread. Turning unison on drops the channel to 1 note/SID,
-- which is exactly what we want here: this lead is meant to sound like one
-- thick oscillator, not three thin ones.
  once $ ccn 41 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 2  -- CC_UNIS: on, normalized 1 -> 127
  once $ ccn 42 # ccv 51  # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 2  -- CC_UDET: moderate spread, normalized 0.4 -> 51
-- SID2 LFO1: slow pitch vibrato, subtle - gives the unison lead some life
-- without turning it into a siren.
  once $ ccn 0 # ccv 0  # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 2  -- CC_LFOW: Triangle, normalized 0.0 -> 0
  once $ ccn 2 # ccv 32 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 2  -- CC_LFOR: slow-ish rate, normalized 0.25 -> 32
  once $ ccn 3 # ccv 38 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 2  -- CC_LFOD: subtle depth, normalized 0.3 -> 38
  once $ ccn 4 # ccv 0  # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 2  -- CC_LFOT: Pitch, normalized 0.0 -> 0
-- SID3 LFO2: square-wave wah on the filter cutoff, deep and syncopated -
-- this is what turns the plain arp'd chord into something that breathes.
  once $ ccn 13 # ccv 76  # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3  -- CC_LFO2W: Square, normalized 0.6 -> 76
  once $ ccn 14 # ccv 38  # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3  -- CC_LFO2R: moderate rate, normalized 0.3 -> 38
  once $ ccn 15 # ccv 89  # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3  -- CC_LFO2D: deep, normalized 0.7 -> 89
  once $ ccn 16 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3  -- CC_LFO2T: Cutoff, normalized 1.0 -> 127
-- SID3 arpeggiator, MIDI-clock-synced (see arp_chord_clocksync.tidal for the
-- full clock-division writeup) - this is what turns the held chord below
-- into the "multi-keypress" shimmer: three real simultaneous note-ons,
-- cycled fast enough by the firmware's own engine to read as one texture.
  once $ ccn 6  # ccv 0   # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3  -- CC_ARPM: Up, normalized 0.0 -> 0
  once $ ccn 11 # ccv 0   # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3  -- CC_ARPO: 0 extra octaves, stay in the triad, normalized 0.0 -> 0
  once $ ccn 10 # ccv 108 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3  -- CC_ARPR: fast clock division, normalized 0.85 -> 108
  once $ ccn 12 # ccv 127 # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3  -- CC_ARPE: on, normalized 1 -> 127

setcps 0.65 -- uncomment and tune to taste - up-tempo, circus-EDM pace

-- The clock - required for CC_ARPR's division to mean anything; without it
-- the arp falls back to its free-running ~20Hz ceiling instead.
do
  once $ midicmd "start" # s "usbsidpico"
  p "midiclock" $ midicmd "midiClock*96" # s "usbsidpico"

-- SID1 bass: pumping eighth-notes, root follows the progression. Patch
-- swaps from Wide bass to the tighter Bass patch exactly at bar 3 - a live
-- Program Change mid-song, not just a boot-time setting.
do
  d1 $ note "<[e2*8] [c2*8] [g2*8] [d2*8]>"
    # legato 1
    # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 1
  d5 $ progNum "<14 14 3 3>" # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 1

-- SID2 unison lead: syncopated riff, one thick detuned voice throughout.
d2 $ note "<[e4 ~ g4 b4] [c4 ~ e4 g4] [g3 ~ b3 d4] [d4 ~ fs4 a4]>"
  # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 2

-- SID3: the held chord the arpeggiator turns into a shimmer - one triad per
-- bar, sustained, so many fast arp steps read as one chord rather than a
-- run. Root-position triads.
d3 $ n "<e'min c'maj g'maj d'maj>"
  # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 3

-- FMOpl hook: syncopated top line, answering the unison lead. Patch swaps
-- from Electric piano to Bell at the same bar-3 drop as the SID1 bass, so
-- both patch changes land together and the section change is unmistakable.
do
  d4 $ note "<[b4 ~ e5 ~ d5 ~ b4 ~] [g4 ~ c5 ~ b4 ~ g4 ~] [d4 ~ g4 ~ fs4 ~ d4 ~] [a4 ~ d5 ~ cs5 ~ a4 ~]>"
    # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 4
  d6 $ progNum "<1 1 4 4>" # s "usbsidpico" # midichan 4

-- To stop: hush
